2. How the DOM Works?

When an HTML document is loaded in the browser:
	1.	The browser reads the HTML code.
	2.	It parses the HTML and creates a DOM tree (a hierarchical representation of the document).
	3.	JavaScript can be used to manipulate this tree (add, remove, or modify elements).
	4.	The changes are reflected in real-time in the web page.

⸻

3. Structure of the DOM

The DOM represents an HTML document as a tree of objects. Each object is called a node.

Example HTML:

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
</body>
</html>

The DOM tree structure for this document looks like this:

Document
 ├── <html>
 │   ├── <head>
 │   │   └── <title>My Page</title>
 │   ├── <body>
 │   │   ├── <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
 │   │   └── <p>This is a paragraph.</p>



⸻

4. Nodes in the DOM

The DOM consists of different types of nodes, which represent various parts of an HTML document.

Node Type	Description
Document Node	Represents the entire document (document object in JavaScript).
Element Node	Represents HTML elements (e.g., <h1>, <p>, <div>).
Attribute Node	Represents attributes of elements (e.g., class="container").
Text Node	Represents the text inside elements (e.g., “Hello, World!”).
Comment Node	Represents HTML comments.



