5. Accessing Elements in the DOM

JavaScript provides various methods to select elements from the DOM.

(a) Using document.getElementById()

Finds an element by its ID.

let title = document.getElementById("myTitle"); 
console.log(title.innerText);

(b) Using document.getElementsByClassName()

Finds elements by class name.

let elements = document.getElementsByClassName("myClass"); 
console.log(elements[0].innerText);

(c) Using document.getElementsByTagName()

Finds elements by tag name.

let paragraphs = document.getElementsByTagName("p");
console.log(paragraphs[0].innerText);

(d) Using document.querySelector()

Finds the first matching element using a CSS selector.

let element = document.querySelector(".myClass"); 
console.log(element.innerText);

(e) Using document.querySelectorAll()

Finds all matching elements using a CSS selector.

let elements = document.querySelectorAll("p");
elements.forEach((el) => console.log(el.innerText));



